!Reel - a video player for RISC OS                        0.1.16
===================================================================

Reel plays video files in a desktop window, with sound. It uses FFmpeg
5.1 (the riscos-ffmpeg port, with its NEON code), so it plays almost
anything: MP4, MKV, WebM, AVI, MOV, MPEG; H.264, HEVC, VP8, VP9, AV1,
MPEG-1/2/4, Theora and more. It needs an ARMv7 machine with NEON
(Raspberry Pi 2/3/4/400, Titanium, IGEPv5, ...).

Using it
--------
Double-click !Reel: its icon (a film reel) appears on the icon bar. Then:

  - drop a video file on the icon, or on Reel's window; or
  - double-click a video file in the Filer (while Reel is loaded).

Drop several files at once (one drag from the Filer) and they play one
after another: a playlist. Hold Shift as you drop to add files to the
playlist instead of starting a new one.

The window shows the picture with a row of controls under it:

  Pause/Play    stop and start (a click on the picture does the same)
  < 10s  10s >  go back or forward 10 seconds
  the bar       click anywhere on it to go there
  the time      where you are / how long the video is; then the speed
                if it isn't normal (e.g. 1.5x), and A or AB while
                A-B repeat is set
  the volume    the green bar: click along it to set the volume.
                Reel remembers it for next time
  Full          full screen (Escape or F to come back)

Keys (click in the window first):
  Space         pause / play
  Left, Right   back / forward 10 seconds
  Down, Up      back / forward 1 minute
  F             full screen on and off
  Escape        leave full screen
  M             the mini player on and off
  D             deinterlace: Auto, On, Off in turn
  A             A-B repeat: set A, then B (it repeats), then off
  N, P          the next / previous file in the playlist
  Q             close the video
  I             the media info window on and off

Resize the window by dragging the grip in its bottom right corner (the
three diagonal lines), or choose a size from Window size on its menu; the
picture keeps its shape. When a playlist is playing, the title shows
which file it is, e.g. "(2/5)".

Menu on the window:
  Media info          the media info window (below)
  Full screen         the whole screen (Escape to come back)
  Mini player         a small player above the icon bar (below)
  Keep on top         the mini player comes back to the front
  Window size >       Half (50%), Actual size (100%), Double (200%) of
                      the video's size, or Fit the screen (as big as
                      fits above the icon bar). Never narrower than the
                      controls. The grip resizes it to any size
  Picture >           Fit: the whole picture, with black bars
                      Fill (crop): fill the window, cutting off the edges
                      Original size: pixel for pixel, centred
                      Stretch: fill the window, out of shape
  Deinterlace >       Auto: only the pictures the file marks as
                      interlaced (TV recordings, DVDs); On: every
                      picture (for files that don't say); Off: never.
                      Remembered
  Speed >             0.5x to 2x; the sound keeps its pitch
  Sound track >       for files with more than one (e.g. languages)
  Playlist >          jump to a file; Clear the rest
  A-B repeat          as the A key
  Loop                play the file (or the playlist) again and again
  Fast decode         cheaper, rougher decoding, for files that are too
                      much for the machine (it skips the deblocking
                      filter, so you may see blocks)
  Vsync (full screen) full screen waits for the screen's refresh before
                      each picture: no tearing. Untick it to draw at
                      once (a little faster; you may see a tear line).
                      *Set Reel$NoVsync 1 starts with it off
  Close               close the video

Menu on the icon: Info (the program's name, author and version), Loop,
Log, Quit. The video's own details are Media info, on the window's menu.

Mini player
-----------
M, or Mini player on the window menu, swaps the window for a small one
with no title bar in the bottom right of the screen, just above the icon
bar: the picture (320 pixels wide on most screens), Play/Pause, the
position bar and Normal. Drag the picture to move it, and the grip in
its bottom right corner to make it bigger or smaller (it keeps the
video's shape); it opens there, at that size, next time. Double-click
the picture, click Normal or press M for the normal window again. The
menu, keys and everything else work as in the window.

The mini player is made to be watched while you do other things:
  - the picture is shrunk properly: a big reduction (a 1280-pixel video
    in a 320-pixel mini player is a quarter of its size) first halves
    the picture, averaging each 2x2 block of pixels, then scales what's
    left, so fine detail stays smooth instead of breaking up into jagged
    edges, for about the same work;
  - it decodes a little less: the deblocking filter is skipped on the
    pictures no other picture is predicted from (about 15% less work for
    typical H.264, and nothing you can see at that size). Going back to
    the normal window turns it off again, with nothing left over. (Fast
    decode on the menu skips it on every picture, in either window.)
Media info's Converting line says when the picture was halved.

Keep on top: RISC OS has no "always on top" for ordinary windows, so
another window can cover the mini player. With Keep on top ticked, the
mini player looks about once a second while playing and, if something
has been opened over it, comes back to the front. It never takes the
keyboard from what you're typing in.

Deinterlacing
-------------
TV recordings, DVDs and camcorder video are often interlaced: each
picture is two half-pictures (fields) taken a moment apart, so anything
moving shows comb-like stripes. Reel removes them with FFmpeg's yadif,
which looks at the pictures before and after to fill in each missing line:
sharp where things are still, smooth where they move. One picture a
frame (25 a second for PAL). On ARM it runs in NEON (added in this port;
it gives exactly the same picture as FFmpeg's plain C). Media info shows
whether the file says it's interlaced (Scan) and, while playing, what
deinterlacing is doing and its cost a picture (Deinterlacing).
With Auto, progressive video (most downloads) costs nothing.

Carrying on where you stopped
-----------------------------
Reel remembers where you stopped each file (the last 100). Open one
again and it asks whether to carry on from there or start again. Files
played to the end, or hardly started, aren't remembered.

Choices
-------
The volume, Deinterlace, Keep on top and where the mini player goes are saved in
<Choices$Write>.Reel.Choices (ReelEGL for !ReelEGL); where files were
stopped in <Choices$Write>.Reel.Resume. The other options are for this
session only.

NEON
----
Besides FFmpeg's own NEON code (H.264, VP8/VP9, AV1 through dav1d,
MPEG-1/2/4, HEVC's brightness), this port adds NEON for:
  - deinterlacing (yadif);
  - HEVC's colour (chroma motion compensation);
  - resizing and colour conversion to the screen whenever the picture
    isn't shown at its own size: full screen, a resized window, the
    mini player, Fill and Stretch.
Each gives exactly the same pictures as FFmpeg's plain C code.

Media info
----------
A window (it stays open while you watch; I or the window menu) with,
at the top, "stats for nerds", updated every second while playing:

  Position, Clock   where the picture is, and the clock it follows (the
                    sound, or a timer), with how far apart they are
  Pictures shown    a second, against the video's own frame rate, and
                    how many were skipped as late
  Decoded           pictures decoded a second, the time each took, and
                    how many times faster than real time that is
  Decoding load     the share of the time spent decoding
  Frame skipping    whether Reel is skipping frames to keep up
  Converting        swscale's time a picture (colours and scaling)
  Deinterlacing     Auto/On/Off, the time a picture, and how many
                    pictures were interlaced
  Drawing           the time to put each picture on the screen
  Waiting           pictures decoded and packets read but not yet used
  Sound             the sound output and how much is queued
  Reading           how fast the file is being read
  Desktop           null events a second, how much of the time Reel
                    was asleep, and the screen mode

and below that what's in the file: the container, length, size and bit
rate; the video's codec, profile, level, size, aspect, frame rate, pixel
format and colours; the sound's codec and profile (e.g. HE-AAC),
channels, sample rate and bit rate; and the sound output used.
Times are measured with the centisecond clock, so they're averages.

Log
---
Reel writes a log as it goes, started afresh each time Reel starts:
<Wimp$ScrapDir>.ReelLog (ReelEGLLog for !ReelEGL). Log on the icon's
menu opens it. It has the sound modules found (and their versions), the
screen mode, each file opened and what's in it, what you did (pause,
seek, full screen), any errors, FFmpeg's messages, and once a second
while playing: the position, the clock, the sound (queued, and
StreamManager's added and played counts) and how many pictures were
shown, and what each picture cost in milliseconds: decoding,
converting (to what size), drawing and deinterlacing - in full screen
too, where Media info can't be seen. If something goes wrong, the log
says what Reel saw.
To put it somewhere else: *Set Reel$Log <file> before starting Reel;
*Set Reel$Log off for none.

Speed
-----
Everything is decoded on the one CPU core. On a Raspberry Pi 4, 720p
H.264 plays well at normal bitrates (up to about 5 Mbit/s); very high
bitrate files (like 25 Mbit/s camera or "HD" downloads) fall behind.
Then Reel keeps up with the sound: 0.3 s behind, it stops decoding the
frames nothing else needs (most B-frames); 1.5 s behind, it decodes only
keyframes until it has caught up. The log says when. To make a file play
smoothly, convert it once, e.g. on a faster computer:

  ffmpeg -i in.mp4 -c:v libx264 -preset slow -tune fastdecode -b:v 3M
         -vf scale=-2:720 -c:a aac -b:a 128k out.mp4

A smaller window is cheaper than full screen: the picture is scaled to
the window.

Between pictures Reel sleeps (Wimp_PollIdle) until the next one is due,
having decoded the next few already, so other programs get the time and
Task Manager shows Reel using about what decoding and drawing need.
*Set Reel$NoSleep 1 (ReelEGL$NoSleep) before starting it polls flat out
instead, as before 0.1.9, to compare.

Needs
-----
Included: PThreadTicker 0.01, a small module from UnixLib 5.0.1 (the C
library Reel is built with) for its thread timer; !Run loads it (a copy
merged into !System first). Reel itself plays on one thread, so it's
there for the cases that start one (SDL's sound). Licence:
docs.Licences.PThreadTicker (BSD).
SharedUnixLibrary 1.16 or later and ARMEABISupport (PackMan). For sound:
SharedSoundBuffer and StreamManager, John Duffell's modules (SharedSound,
which they use, is part of RISC OS). Download ssb.zip from Andrew
Sellors' RDPClient page:
  https://orac.co.uk/software/rdpclient/rdpclient.html
and merge its !System into yours; Reel's !Run loads them. John Duffell's
own site (now on the Internet Archive) has more details:
  https://web.archive.org/web/20110920080106/http://www.duffell.riscos.me.uk/
Without them Reel plays the picture, keeps time itself, and Media info
says why there is no sound.
Reel gives the sound straight to SharedSoundBuffer (from 0.1.5; before,
it went through SDL). If the stream opens but doesn't play (for about a
second nothing it was given is used up), Reel carries on without sound;
Media info then says "the device isn't playing". To try SDL's sound
instead: *Set REELCORE_AUDIO sdl before starting Reel.

!ReelEGL
--------
The zip also has !ReelEGL: the same player, drawing through riscos-mesa's
EGL (an example program for EGL; full screen can write straight into
screen memory). See its !Help.

Known limits
------------
- One video at a time (a playlist plays them in turn). No subtitles yet.
- Files with no video (sound only) aren't played.

